Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"14rw87f": "Dietary supplements",
"1thgjsc": "What has evidence and what does not: vitamin D, omega-3, multivitamins and more.",
"1k2r254": "Dietary supplements are sold as a shortcut to a long life, but in the large trials almost none of them extended life in healthy people. There are exceptions, mainly when a deficiency is measured in a blood test.",
"ewi116": "In the VITAL trial, vitamin D did not change overall mortality (HR 0.99)",
"spy95j": "In Cochrane, omega-3 barely changed overall mortality (RR 0.97)",
"wtzolc": "Mortality",
"qjijzt": "In high-quality trials, antioxidant supplements slightly raised mortality",
"1ud1br4": "The first rule: a supplement fills a deficiency",
"19y67uz": "The important distinction is between a deficiency and an extra. For someone missing a vitamin or mineral, a supplement corrects the deficiency, and that can help. Someone without a deficiency gains almost no years in the large trials. So the first step is not to buy a supplement, but to check with a blood test and talk to a doctor.",
"12azu85": "Vitamin D",
"12dnaoz": "In the VITAL trial (Manson 2019), 25,871 participants received vitamin D or placebo for a median of 5.3 years. No difference was found in cancer (HR 0.96, 95% CI: 0.88–1.06), in cardiovascular events (HR 0.97, 0.85–1.12), or in death from any cause (HR 0.99, 0.87–1.12). Someone with a deficiency found in a blood test should decide on a supplement together with their doctor.",
"fgbh3n": "Omega-3 (fish oil)",
"1sl7d6t": "In the Cochrane review (Abdelhamid 2020), 86 trials with 162,796 participants, omega-3 supplements barely changed overall mortality (RR 0.97, 95% CI: 0.93–1.01, high certainty). There was a small reduction in death from heart disease and in coronary events (RR 0.91 for coronary events), but certainty was low to moderate: about 167 people would need to be treated to prevent one coronary event.",
"1e3iy9x": "Multivitamins and antioxidants",
"1ktv5tk": "The USPSTF recommendation (2022) concluded, with moderate certainty, that the harms of beta-carotene outweigh its benefits in preventing heart disease and cancer, and that vitamin E offers no net benefit. For multivitamins and other single supplements, the evidence is insufficient to say whether benefits outweigh harms.",
"wck88q": "In Bjelakovic's meta-analysis (2007), 47 high-quality trials with 180,938 participants, antioxidant supplements slightly raised mortality (RR 1.05, 95% CI: 1.02–1.08). Beta-carotene (RR 1.07), vitamin A (RR 1.16) and vitamin E (RR 1.04) raised mortality, alone or combined. No effect on mortality was found for vitamin C or selenium. This is a 2007 study.",
"1fjjo63": "Vitamin B12: when yes",
"1q3w6if": "B12 is a case where testing and a supplement are sometimes justified. In the DPPOS trial (Aroda 2016), among people who took metformin for years, low B12 (up to 203 pg/mL) was more common than in the placebo group after 5 years (4.3% vs 2.3%), and low or borderline B12 was even more common (19.1% vs 9.5%). Each year of treatment added risk (OR 1.13 per year). The researchers recommended considering routine B12 testing for people on metformin. If a low value is found, the doctor will decide on treatment and dose.",
"16pj2pd": "Creatine: evidence for muscle, not for longevity",
"1udt0kw": "In Chilibeck's meta-analysis (2017), 22 trials with 721 participants aged 57–70 on average, creatine supplementation alongside resistance training increased lean muscle mass by 1.37 kg (95% CI: 0.97–1.76) and slightly increased strength. This is a measure of muscle, not of mortality: the studies did not test whether it extends life. The training itself is what matters most.",
"11bf7mm": "What about NR, NMN, resveratrol and others",
"y1esyz": "Supplements such as nicotinamide riboside (NR) are marketed as anti-aging supplements. In Martens' trial (2018), two 6-week periods in healthy middle-aged and older adults, NR was well tolerated and raised NAD+ metabolism. This is a biological marker, and the study did not look at mortality or disease. The researchers themselves write that further trials are needed to test benefit, for example on blood pressure.",
"1qcp4t": "Safety",
"joe26b": "A supplement is not safe just because it is called natural. Supplements can affect medications and underlying conditions, and they usually do not go through efficacy approval the way a drug does. Before you start, especially with regular medication, a chronic condition, pregnancy or breastfeeding, it is worth asking a doctor or pharmacist.",
"wwreve": "Do not buy a supplement just in case",
"s8wrg9": "First a blood test and a talk with a doctor.",
"1gmq311": "Ask the doctor about B12 if you take metformin",
"i94ho8": "It is a simple test that the study recommends considering.",
"ucwah8": "Invest first in habits with evidence",
"1gox6gn": "Sleep, movement, nutrition: see the guides.",
"4a5ghf": "Check what is written on the supplement",
"vt86o4": "A claim like \"extends life\" requires research in humans, not only in mice.",
"untml": "A multivitamin just in case can't hurt",
"1dlneh8": "The USPSTF (2022) found the evidence on multivitamins insufficient, and found harm from beta-carotene and no benefit from vitamin E.",
"1rt5rh0": "Vitamin D extends life",
"th1xy4": "In VITAL (25,871 participants) there was no difference in overall mortality (HR 0.99).",
"bhtuga": "Not included in the calculator: there is no established hazard ratio for dietary supplements, so the calculator does not assume that taking them adds years.",
"64i8q0": "Manson 2019: vitamin D and prevention of cancer and heart disease (NEJM, VITAL)",
"7wavc0": "Abdelhamid 2020: omega-3 and heart disease (Cochrane)",
"inq466": "Bjelakovic 2007: antioxidants and mortality (JAMA)",
"1h9bdo6": "USPSTF 2022: vitamins and multivitamins for preventing heart disease and cancer (JAMA)",
"1cq792p": "Aroda 2016: metformin and B12 deficiency (J Clin Endocrinol Metab)",
"1l6pj98": "Chilibeck 2017: creatine and muscle in older adults (Open Access J Sports Med)",
"1uf6wue": "Martens 2018: nicotinamide riboside in older adults (Nat Commun)"
});
