Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"18izyzr": "Biological age",
"13t28g4": "Biological age: how is it calculated from blood tests? PhenoAge",
"suket0": "How biological age is calculated from nine blood values, and how well it predicts.",
"svvpzt": "Biological age (PhenoAge) is calculated from your age and nine values in a blood test. Each year it is higher than your actual age was linked to a 9% higher mortality risk, a hazard ratio of 1.09.",
"w7p1th": "Biological age is a single number that summarizes how \"old\" your physiology looks on a routine blood test, relative to your actual age. It is not a measure of the clock but of risk: someone whose number is above their age is in a group with higher mortality.",
"1ma588o": "blood tests",
"1xkgifa": "go into the formula, together with age",
"1bvrulb": "mortality for each year that the PhenoAge is higher (confidence interval 1.08–1.10)",
"ev9a5t": "in predicting 10-year mortality, versus 0.855 for age alone",
"13siidq": "What goes into the calculation",
"swz28l": "Levine's formula uses age and nine values from a routine blood test: albumin, creatinine, glucose, CRP, lymphocyte percentage, MCV, RDW, alkaline phosphatase and the white blood cell count (WBC). The weights were derived from a survival model on American data, and the whole formula has been published, including its 2019 correction.",
"1lft197": "How well it predicts",
"19fy1ix": "In Liu's study (PLoS Med 2018), each year of PhenoAge above age was linked to a hazard ratio of 1.09 (confidence interval 1.08–1.10) for all-cause mortality. In predicting 10-year mortality, the AUC of PhenoAge alone was 0.879, versus 0.855 for age alone. So it adds to age, but modestly.",
"18b7bto": "It is not a diagnosis and not a measure of your \"true age\". It is a statistical measure of groups: people with the same value are on average at a certain risk, and an individual can be far from the average. Values vary from test to test, and a test taken during acute illness, for example with a high CRP, will distort the result.",
"6ilufh": "How it enters the calculator",
"892keh": "The calculator computes biological age only when all nine values from a 12-month window are present. You can upload a blood-test file and the values are filled in locally in your browser. The difference from actual age is limited to 12 years in either direction, and it enters as one factor. When it exists, kidney function and CRP are not counted separately, so that they are not counted twice.",
"1rp3e2g": "Check that all nine values are in the test",
"e5xvl1": "If a value is missing, biological age will not be calculated.",
"i3tub": "Use a test from the last year",
"hre9gf": "Older values are not included in the calculation.",
"vtyplv": "Do not rely on a test taken during illness",
"9jhprd": "Wait for a calm period, mainly because of CRP.",
"14823ss": "Focus on habits, not the number",
"1bktv9p": "A high number is a reason to check with a doctor and to improve sleep, activity and weight.",
"1o97e73": "My biological age is my true age",
"12qw7gc": "It is a risk measure based on group averages, not a measurement of the body's \"age\".",
"1ivarkf": "A low biological age guarantees a long life",
"8eaunc": "PhenoAge predicted mortality only slightly better than age alone (AUC 0.879 versus 0.855), and it is an estimate for groups.",
"ngsr7u": "Included in the calculator: biological age from nine blood values within the last year, at a hazard ratio of 1.09 per year, with the difference limited to 12 years.",
"14zwrbr": "Levine 2018: biological age from blood tests (Aging)",
"1snpe8f": "Liu 2018: phenotypic age and mortality prediction (PLoS Med)",
"cowvx1": "Liu 2019: correction to the formula (PLoS Med)"
});
Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"1f8zuux": "How excess weight is defined",
"1efiq8o": "According to the World Health Organization, a BMI below 18.5 is underweight, 18.5 to 24.9 is the normal range, 25 to 29.9 is overweight, and 30 or more is obesity: grade 1 (30–34.9), grade 2 (35–39.9) and grade 3 (40 and above). This is the division used in the study, so the numbers here refer to these groups.",
"1hkcl82": "How to read a hazard ratio",
"1gdkjva": "A hazard ratio of 1.12 means that at every age, the death rate in the group is 12% higher than in the group within the normal range. It does not mean that 12% of people will die. The closer the ratio is to 1, the smaller the difference; a ratio of 2.68 means 2.68 times the rate.",
"gyir3u": "Men and women",
"1bmkds4": "The difference between the sexes grows with the grade of obesity. With mild overweight the ratio was 1.12 in men and 1.08 in women; in grade 1 it was 1.70 in men and 1.37 in women; in grade 2 it was 2.68 and 1.86; and in grade 3 it was 4.24 and 2.73.",
"1nrslzt": "An example of reading",
"hmtd34": "A man with a BMI of 32 is in grade 1 obesity, where the ratio was 1.70 compared with the normal range. A woman with the same BMI: 1.37. These are group hazard ratios and not a personal forecast, and the calculator combines them with age, smoking, blood tests and the other factors.",
"1m33y86": "What the study did not examine",
"5rgwkd": "The study followed people over time and did not examine what happens to those who lose weight. So it cannot be concluded from it that losing weight will reduce the risk by a certain amount. The estimate in years in the calculator assumes that someone whose weight falls takes on the risk of someone at that weight, and that is an assumption.",
"1jr3ae0": "When to talk to a doctor",
"1lnho79": "It is worth talking to a doctor when the BMI is 30 or more, when weight rises or falls quickly for no clear reason, or when excess weight comes together with high blood pressure or high blood sugar. A doctor can assess the whole picture and suggest suitable treatment.",
"1bg3jmi": "Why smokers and the sick are filtered out",
"17ssk87": "Low weight is often linked to high mortality, but not always because of the weight: smoking makes people leaner, and serious illness causes weight loss. If these people are left in the analysis, heavier people look healthier than they really are. So the study excluded those who smoked and those with a chronic disease, and also the first five years of follow-up. This reduces the bias, but does not remove it completely.",
"1q71vj8": "Waist circumference adds information",
"stppda": "In a pooled study by Cerhan (Mayo Clin Proc 2014), every additional 5 cm of waist circumference was linked to an increase of about 7% to 9% in mortality risk. The figure was checked against the study abstract only. That is why the calculator also asks about waist circumference: it helps to tell apart people with the same BMI.",
"oqb44p": "BMI enters according to the GBMC curve. In men the curve is adjusted upward from a BMI of 21, so that it matches the ratios reported for men. In the \"what if\" simulator you can move the weight and see how many years a change of a few kilograms is worth in your case. Any result in years is the model's estimate.",
"1ev67u3": "Cerhan 2014: waist circumference and mortality (Mayo Clin Proc)",
"8yh0y3": "World Health Organization: obesity and overweight",
"1dxr677": "What each value reflects",
"a0uive": "Each of the nine values says something different about the body, roughly: albumin about nutrition and inflammation, creatinine about kidney function, glucose about sugar metabolism, CRP about inflammation, lymphocyte percentage and the white cell count (WBC) about the immune system, MCV and RDW about the size of red blood cells and the variation between them, and alkaline phosphatase about the liver and bones. The formula did not choose them by biological explanation but by what predicted mortality in the data.",
"rw2xi5": "How to calculate it in practice",
"6p6jd7": "You need a blood test that includes the nine values, usually a complete blood count and chemistry tests, plus CRP. CRP and RDW sometimes have to be requested explicitly, because they are not always part of a routine test. In the calculator you can upload the test file, and it is read in the browser only, or type the values in manually.",
"hr6z2h": "How to read the result",
"15a9wld": "If biological age equals actual age, the risk is average for your age. If it is higher, the risk is higher, and if it is lower, the risk is lower. At a hazard ratio of 1.09 per year, a gap of 5 years is a risk multiple of about 1.5, and a gap of 10 years is a multiple of about 2.4. This is arithmetic that assumes the ratio holds across the range, not a separate finding from the study.",
"7eg30t": "If biological age is 55 and actual age is 50, the gap is 5 years, and in the model that is a risk multiple of about 1.5, before the weakening of the effect with age and the weighting of the other factors.",
"v5jmwa": "What the formula does not include",
"1jzmh1b": "The formula does not include smoking, blood pressure, weight or fitness. Some of them also act through the blood values, so the calculator gives related factors reduced weight so as not to count them twice.",
"1diag5l": "What to do with the result",
"1awvxn9": "If biological age is higher than actual age, it is worth checking which value is out of range and discussing it with a doctor. The values themselves are linked to familiar habits such as weight, sugar, activity and sleep, and the guides on the site cover each of them. A single number is a starting point, not a verdict, and a trend across several tests may be more informative.",
"1da4d36": "Why the difference is limited",
"1my3cpr": "Extreme blood values, for example because of acute illness, can produce a biological age very far from the actual age. To limit the effect of an outlying value, the calculator limits the difference to 12 years in each direction. This is our limit, not the study's.",
"1wackbv": "Other measures of biological age",
"ospn2x": "There are other measures called \"biological age\", including epigenetic clocks based on chemical marks on DNA. They are not included in the calculator, and we have not examined them. PhenoAge was chosen because it is calculated from blood tests that most people already take, and its formula has been published in full."
});
Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"1o0ruzy": "Life expectancy in Israel",
"ry5pv3": "Life expectancy in Israel by age and sex: the latest figures",
"rb707y": "Life expectancy in Israel according to the CBS and WHO, and what it means at each age.",
"1n4j6pi": "According to the Central Bureau of Statistics (CBS), life expectancy in Israel in 2023 was 81.0 years for men and 85.5 for women. Excluding those killed in the Iron Swords war, it was 81.7 and 85.7.",
"e8ekdd": "Life expectancy is a group average, not a forecast for a particular person. To understand it you need to know which figure you are looking at: who measured it, for which year, and at which age.",
"segeds": "life expectancy at birth of men in Israel in 2023, according to the CBS",
"sdi03y": "life expectancy at birth of women in Israel in 2023, according to the CBS",
"1md2y4x": "the gap in favor of women in 2023 (85.5 minus 81.0)",
"fgue9j": "The latest CBS figures",
"2e34zy": "According to the CBS (Israel in Figures 2024), life expectancy at birth in 2023 was 81.0 years for men and 85.5 for women, compared with 80.7 and 84.8 in 2022. The CBS also notes that if those killed in the Iron Swords war are not counted, life expectancy was 81.7 for men and 85.7 for women.",
"oik9rt": "Why the numbers differ between sources",
"17jc0wo": "The World Health Organization publishes a table for Israel for 2019, in which life expectancy at birth is 80.9 for men and 84.4 for women. The difference from the CBS stems mainly from a different year, and may also stem from the calculation method. The media quote different numbers also because of the difference between counting those killed in the war and not counting them. It is always worth checking the year, source and method.",
"jmd1on": "Life expectancy by age",
"gdk914": "Life expectancy at birth is an average that also includes those who died young. Someone who has already reached a certain age has passed the early risks, so their expected age is higher. According to the WHO table for Israel (2019), at age 30 an average of about 51.6 years remain for men and about 54.8 for women. At age 50: 32.5 and 35.3. At age 65: 19.6 and 21.6. At age 80: 8.9 and 9.6.",
"9o1mcf": "At ages 60 and 85 these are values that the WHO publishes directly: 23.7 and 26.0 years remain at age 60, and 6.1 and 6.4 at age 85. The other ages are calculated from the same table.",
"op1ia4": "The gap between men and women",
"1k7rr3p": "The gap between the sexes in 2023 was about 4.5 years in favor of women. The gap shrinks with age: at age 80 it is about 0.7 years according to the table.",
"1l7l771": "What these numbers do not say",
"506oz9": "A population's life expectancy does not take into account your habits, tests and family history. The CBS also publishes further breakdowns, for example by population group and region, and they are not included here because we have not examined them. To see what the number is for you, the calculator starts from the life table for your age and sex and adjusts it to habits and tests.",
"dl6rwk": "Check the figure at your age",
"hhkzgb": "Use the breakdown by age, not life expectancy at birth.",
"1sxedqf": "Calculate it personally",
"1xn8ev2": "The calculator adjusts the table to habits and tests.",
"1aap3y3": "Check the latest CBS publication",
"1tvn7yp": "The figures are updated every year.",
"19i4orp": "Life expectancy at birth is the age at which a person will die",
"1iiwiqi": "It is a group average. Someone who reached 65 is expected to live on average to 84.6 if a man and 86.6 if a woman, more than life expectancy at birth.",
"v6nn07": "Men and women have the same life expectancy",
"jaxrj0": "In 2023 the gap was about 4.5 years in favor of women.",
"1cfm58i": "Included in the calculator: the WHO life table for Israel (2019) as the baseline by age and sex. The updated CBS figures are not yet used in the calculation.",
"nkky7u": "CBS: Israel in Figures 2024 (life expectancy 2023)",
"1jo3uv5": "World Health Organization: life tables (GHO)"
});
