Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"1qvu5ej": "To the smoking-cessation coach: a personal plan",
"weetb": "Smoking-cessation coach",
"14xppzp": "A few questions about you, and a research-based plan for quitting smoking, with sources. No judgment.",
"131qzsc": "This is not a diagnosis or medical advice. We do not recommend a specific prescription drug or doses. When in doubt, ask a doctor.",
"13oyme5": "The coach requires JavaScript. You can read the habits guide instead.",
"j93cvq": "To the full habits guide, with all the sources",
"1wlhqni": "Roughly how many cigarettes a day?",
"153d27f": "Fewer than 10",
"1oyxci": "More than 20",
"qsyl1u": "How soon after waking do you smoke your first cigarette?",
"1bawlnz": "Within half an hour",
"47i80o": "More than half an hour",
"13dsdkz": "Have you tried to quit before?",
"1jt116m": "Yes, and I went back to smoking",
"130dz8c": "Yes, and I had severe withdrawal symptoms",
"frs0d": "What is making you think about this now? You can tick several.",
"1c4rqt4": "My health",
"384tpg": "Money",
"3su9bj": "My family",
"1am34al": "Another reason",
"gv9u8i": "When is it hardest without a cigarette? You can tick several.",
"eqf7j9": "After a meal",
"bynh37": "With coffee",
"zf3dpq": "Under stress",
"1mgscxr": "Socialising or drinking alcohol",
"ahgefy": "Boredom",
"1vw8kp": "Driving",
"177vt8h": "I am pregnant, planning a pregnancy or breastfeeding",
"yt7khf": "I have heart disease, or I have had a cardiac event",
"1nargym": "I take psychiatric medication or am being treated for a mental-health condition",
"r0j5hx": "I have another chronic illness",
"1x4oyye": "How ready are you to quit?",
"vaax4r": "I have already decided",
"zflpqe": "Thinking about it",
"1834vj": "Just curious",
"p52sq8": "After a meal: get up from the table right away and do something else, like brushing your teeth or washing dishes.",
"dnpqe2": "With coffee: change the habit (tea, a different place, a different time), because coffee is a regular trigger.",
"19nd0b4": "Under stress: prepare a different response in advance for the stressful moment, such as a short walk, water or a few slow breaths.",
"9md2uj": "Socialising or drinking: tell friends you are quitting, and at first avoid the situations where it is hardest.",
"2mojq9": "Boredom: plan a small activity in advance, such as a walk or a call to a friend.",
"iefk7h": "Driving: take the cigarettes and lighter out of the car and keep gum or something else within reach.",
"1iyzka4": "During pregnancy and breastfeeding it is worth consulting a doctor or nurse before any treatment. Counselling is recommended, and for medications there is currently not enough evidence of safety or benefit in pregnancy.",
"1e395fx": "Heart disease",
"b8xtq3": "If you have heart disease, get medical advice before nicotine replacement or medication. In the Cochrane review, nicotine replacement was linked to more chest pain and palpitations.",
"19rv3dz": "Mental health",
"1gi3gag": "If you take psychiatric medication or are being treated for a mental-health condition, talk to your doctor before you start, so they can support you through the process.",
"bncajp": "Chronic illness",
"1q1kh1h": "With a chronic illness it is worth involving a doctor from the start, so that the steps and treatment fit your situation.",
"vb3ahl": "Worth involving a doctor from the start",
"puc954": "Based on your answers, it is worth involving a doctor from the start. This is not a diagnosis, just general advice when smoking is heavy or an earlier attempt was difficult.",
"158xsu": "There is no need to decide right now. You can start by getting to know the options and come back when you are ready.",
"i45p4j": "The decision is already yours, and the next steps help turn it into a plan.",
"1i0q97t": "Thinking about it is a first step. These are the steps worth knowing.",
"ot94sr": "Do not do it alone",
"4wotmw": "Professional support raises the chance of quitting. In Cochrane reviews, individual counselling was about 1.6 times as effective as minimal contact, and combining support with medication was about 1.8 times as effective as brief advice or usual care. These are ratios of the chance of success, not the success rate itself. You can choose what suits you: a session, a group or phone calls.",
"hyqgmq": "Telephone quitline: ",
"jq3wcu": "You can ask your health fund about quit-smoking support services.",
"xrs4e5": "Nicotine replacement",
"1fb59l1": "Nicotine replacement (patches, gum, lozenges) raises the chance of quitting by about 1.5 times. Combining a patch with a fast-acting form worked better than a single form. It is worth asking a pharmacist or doctor how to use them.",
"cd565p": "Talking to a doctor about medication",
"9sbuqq": "Prescription medications for quitting also exist. In reviews they were found effective, and some more effective than nicotine replacement alone, but they suit only some people, so this is a conversation with a doctor. We do not recommend a specific drug or dose.",
"tyfuin": "Planning for the hard moments",
"1nzn7ti": "Most of the difficulty comes at regular moments. It is worth deciding in advance what you will do instead of smoking. These are practical ideas, not research findings:",
"1koc7r5": "Pick two or three hard moments and prepare a different response for each.",
"1sdeft3": "If you slip",
"1ixgp3t": "A slip does not erase what you have achieved so far, and you can try again. It is worth talking to a counsellor or doctor: medications have also been tested for preventing relapse, whereas an additional stand-alone behavioural programme has not been shown to add anything.",
"e9ykaw": "What it is worth",
"91emqe": "In a study by Jha (NEJM, 2013), people who quit smoking at age 45–54 gained about 6 years, and those who quit before age 40 avoided about 90% of the excess risk.",
"1i69bwy": "What motivated you: ",
"19yrz0a": "Your health",
"5qzjbf": "Questions about e-cigarettes or a specific medication: it is worth talking to a doctor. We do not recommend them here.",
"18u3tcv": "Smoking-cessation coach: a research-based personal plan",
"1xsik7r": "A few questions about your smoking, and a research-based quit plan with sources. All in the browser, nothing saved and your answers are never sent."
});
Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"bv9mnc": "To the weight and nutrition coach: a personal plan",
"2qoi3c": "Weight and nutrition coach",
"1pwfskb": "A few questions about you, and a research-based plan for nutrition and moderate weight loss, with sources. No judgment and no promises.",
"uel96m": "This is not a diagnosis or medical or nutritional advice. We do not give calorie targets, medications or doses. When in doubt, ask a doctor or dietitian.",
"1e56l1j": "The coach requires JavaScript. You can read the weight guide instead.",
"clgzz4": "To the full weight guide, with all the sources",
"14b804b": "Height in centimetres (you can skip)",
"jvymya": "Weight in kilograms (you can skip)",
"1x2zbss": "What is the goal?",
"r3aazs": "Lose weight",
"15klg98": "Improve nutrition, without focusing on weight",
"nti141": "Maintain my weight",
"oagxk9": "Not sure",
"16r7vm9": "Have you tried to lose weight before?",
"1n18kyb": "Yes, and the weight came back",
"qetpcd": "Yes, and I kept the result",
"1cw6bvk": "How many sweetened drinks (cola, juice, energy drinks) do you usually have per day?",
"1dsz39": "Almost none",
"1cqh8fw": "One or two",
"yeats1": "Three or more",
"8pcqf4": "How many alcoholic drinks per week?",
"fgwoi": "None",
"zff769": "Up to 7",
"cyrz6v": "More than 7",
"1a9x95i": "How often do you eat fast food, snacks or packaged ready-made food?",
"w6zthy": "Rarely",
"uc0u3y": "A few times a week",
"8d64uy": "Almost every day",
"nxguz3": "How many minutes of physical activity per week (brisk walking, running, cycling)?",
"6htzfk": "Less than 150",
"1jw0lc3": "More than 300",
"1vatc03": "Do you do strength training?",
"pgm0v7": "I am under 18",
"19zb6z2": "I have diabetes and I am treated with insulin or a specific diabetes medication",
"1jdvtja": "I have kidney disease",
"xztbw5": "I lost weight quickly and for no clear reason",
"2nn4is": "I have or had a serious difficulty with food, such as binge eating, vomiting, extreme restriction or compulsive calorie counting",
"1g74edv": "This coach is for adults",
"169gqwf": "This coach is for adults only. For younger people it is worth consulting a paediatrician or a dietitian.",
"1gyeav0": "Low weight",
"1ndyx1t": "Based on the height and weight you entered, your weight is below the normal range. In this situation weight loss is not recommended, and there are also medical reasons for low weight. It is worth talking to a doctor before any change.",
"c8550e": "Difficulty with food",
"97p0c6": "If you have or had a difficulty with food, restrictive diets and calorie counting can be harmful. We do not show a weight-loss plan here. You can turn to a doctor, a dietitian or a therapist who specialises in this. If it is urgent, contact emergency services.",
"167n90x": "Diabetes and medication",
"i2ilxd": "When treated with insulin or a specific diabetes medication, a change in diet or weight loss may require adjusting the treatment. Do not change it on your own. It is worth consulting your treating doctor first.",
"j94f0a": "During pregnancy and breastfeeding, tailored recommendations are needed. We do not show a weight-loss plan here. It is worth consulting a doctor or a dietitian.",
"xaa6kv": "Kidney disease",
"1kkkib1": "With kidney disease there are dietary limits, mainly around protein, that need individual adjustment. It is worth consulting a doctor or a kidney dietitian before changing anything.",
"fv2bea": "Rapid weight loss for no reason",
"w6zg5f": "Unintentional weight loss with no clear reason is a reason for a medical check. This coach is not suitable for that situation.",
"18kccn9": "Based on your answers. This is general research-based information, not a diagnosis and not a substitute for a doctor or dietitian.",
"1q7k5rd": "First: it is worth talking to a professional",
"1lwcwvx": "For this reason we do not show a plan. This coach is general and does not replace personal advice.",
"1a930g4": "Professional advice can help",
"ebeo5q": "At a higher weight, professional advice may help, including structured programmes with support and other options. You can use the suggestions here in addition to a conversation with a doctor, not instead of one.",
"11sk6ek": "Start with a small goal",
"1wjuheo": "You can start small. In studies, a loss of 3% to 5% of body weight, if it is maintained, is already linked to improvements in blood sugar, triglycerides and blood pressure. Loss usually starts quickly and slows after about six months, which is normal and not a failure.",
"186hxfv": "British health guidance gives half a kilogram up to one kilogram per week as a reasonable pace, and warns against sudden weight loss on diets. There is no need to rush.",
"1w9jzmt": "Nutrition you can stick with",
"uxy2sj": "There is no evidence that one diet wins. In a Cochrane review comparing low-carbohydrate with balanced diets, the difference in weight loss was small, about a kilogram or less. What matters is a pattern that suits you and that you can keep up over time.",
"1xqyt8d": "In the PREDIMED trial, people at high cardiovascular risk who ate a Mediterranean diet with extra-virgin olive oil or nuts had fewer heart attacks and strokes. The trial was not designed to reduce weight, so we do not promise weight loss, but it is a pattern with evidence for heart health.",
"1jemybn": "The guidelines recommend at least 150 minutes a week of moderate activity, such as brisk walking. To keep weight off over the years, 200 to 300 minutes a week is mentioned. You can start with 10 minutes and add more.",
"haa6p3": "When people lose weight, part of the loss is muscle mass. In a trial in older adults with excess weight, those who added strength training lost less muscle mass (2% to 3% compared with 5% with aerobic exercise alone). We have no verified figure for the amount of protein during a diet, so it is worth consulting a dietitian.",
"1nic0cr": "Movement and strength training",
"7aftep": "People who usually drank 1–2 sweetened drinks a day had a 26% higher risk of developing type 2 diabetes (observational studies). Replacing them with water, soda water or unsweetened tea is one of the easiest steps to start with.",
"1e6dy6m": "Alcohol has about 7 calories per gram. A glass of wine (175 ml) can contain up to 158 calories, and a pint of beer up to 222. The link between drinking and weight gain in studies is not consistent, and it is clearer with heavy drinking.",
"ahjxc2": "Drinks",
"1joythr": "Ultra-processed food, with caution",
"m1ylqd": "In a small inpatient trial of 20 adults, those who ate a diet high in ultra-processed food ate about 500 more calories a day and gained about 0.9 kg in two weeks. Most other evidence is low-quality observational studies, and there is not yet proof that the processing itself is the cause. You can notice how much of your food is ready-made, packaged and easy to eat a lot of.",
"13rne3h": "Keeping the weight off",
"5kpby2": "Losing the weight is the easy half. The guidelines recommend a maintenance programme of at least a year, with frequent weighing and more physical activity. If you tried before and the weight came back, that is a well-known pattern and not a flaw in you.",
"aopy1w": "Worth noticing",
"guzy7q": "Meal-replacement diets of about 800 calories a day are done only in a medical setting with monitoring, according to the AHA guideline. Do not try this on your own.",
"uizurq": "Medications for obesity also exist. They are prescribed only by a doctor. According to the AHA/ACC/TOS guideline (expert opinion), a medication may be considered as an addition to lifestyle change when BMI is 30 or above, or 27 or above with a related medical condition. This is not a personal recommendation, and we do not recommend a medication or a dose.",
"out4t5": "This coach is general and does not replace personal advice. If you have a medical condition, take medications, or are not sure, it is worth consulting a doctor or dietitian before making a significant change to your diet.",
"z4uyt4": "Weight and nutrition coach: a research-based personal plan",
"1gimdn9": "A few questions about you, and a research-based plan for nutrition and moderate weight loss, with sources. All in the browser, nothing saved and your answers are never sent."
});
Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"19u6dio": "To the alcohol-reduction coach: a personal plan",
"7w7rsk": "Alcohol-reduction coach",
"1emw5ka": "A few questions about you, and a research-based plan for cutting down drinking, with sources. No judgment. This is a tool for cutting down, not a withdrawal programme.",
"11sx4t3": "This is not a diagnosis or medical advice. We do not give withdrawal instructions, medications or doses. Anyone who drinks heavily is referred to a doctor. When in doubt, ask a doctor.",
"52p3pe": "Roughly how many alcoholic drinks per week? (A drink = a glass of wine, a small bottle of beer or a shot of spirits)",
"1oyxcn": "More than 25",
"1u6jdr8": "How often do you have six or more drinks on one occasion?",
"fhdrv": "Never",
"yf8pen": "About once a week",
"13w0fq9": "Every day or almost every day",
"1xkgeun": "In the past, when you cut down or stopped drinking, did any of these happen to you: shaking hands, sweating, strong anxiety, nausea, hallucinations, confusion or a seizure?",
"18mr5nl": "Do you sometimes drink in the morning to calm shaking or anxiety, or to start the day?",
"m5zkpv": "I am pregnant or planning a pregnancy",
"h9dcqh": "I take prescription medication, especially for sleep, anxiety, pain or epilepsy",
"1dtjyun": "I currently have severe depression or anxiety",
"7lmajq": "I have thoughts of harming myself",
"18458pp": "Based on your answers. This is a tool for cutting down, not a withdrawal programme. It is general information, not a diagnosis and not a substitute for a doctor.",
"1ab3dr2": "If you have thoughts of harming yourself",
"matuc1": "You are not alone, and help exists. Reach out now to someone close to you or to a doctor. If there is immediate danger, go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"weo9ce": "Pregnancy",
"16j9c4t": "If you are pregnant or planning a pregnancy, the British guideline says the safest approach is not to drink at all. If that is hard, tell a doctor or midwife. It is a normal part of the conversation, and there is nothing to be ashamed of.",
"1dvv99h": "Before cutting down: it is worth talking to a doctor",
"adxsf": "From what you told us, your body may be used to alcohol. For some people, cutting down sharply or stopping suddenly can cause shaking, sweating, confusion, hallucinations and even seizures. For someone who has had this before, the risk is higher. This does not mean something is wrong with you. It means a doctor should accompany the process, so that it is safe.",
"194ucbr": "So we do not show a do-it-yourself plan for cutting down here. The best step now is to book an appointment with your family doctor and tell them honestly how much you drink. You deserve help without being judged. The doctor can also explain whether medications could help, and that is the doctor's decision, not this site's.",
"1ylj1ev": "In the meantime, do not suddenly stop on your own if you drink a lot every day.",
"eotx5d": "Strong shaking, confusion, hallucinations or a seizure are an emergency. Go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"1sg0lp": "Questions you skipped",
"4a4upt": "You skipped the question about how you felt when you cut down, or about drinking in the morning. If in the past, when you cut down, you felt shaking, strong sweating, confusion or a seizure, do not cut down alone. Talk to a doctor first.",
"121eqtz": "It is worth talking to a doctor",
"16mpgft": "If you take prescription medication, or have severe depression or anxiety, it is worth talking to your doctor about cutting down, because drinking and medication can affect each other.",
"1ohc0rr": "Your consumption is relatively low. Here are a few tools in case you still want to cut down.",
"1ejyxdk": "You can start small. These are steps worth knowing.",
"141kzgl": "A limit in advance, and tracking",
"jzzchg": "In an analysis of 34 studies (15,197 participants), a brief conversation about drinking reduced consumption by about 20 grams of alcohol a week on average after a year. That is a modest step, but it shows that even a small change works. You can set a limit before going out, and write down at the end of the day how much you drank. We do not know whether writing it down helps by itself, but it is a convenient basis for change.",
"1xfps5t": "A reasonable limit, spread out",
"ox46w": "According to the British guideline, it is best not to exceed 14 units a week (about 112 grams of pure alcohol), and to spread drinking over at least three days. In the Wood study (Lancet 2018), mortality risk was lowest up to about 100 grams a week. Less is better.",
"177qz05": "A few fixed days a week without alcohol, for example days that are easy for you. This is a common recommendation in guidelines, not a study proving it is better than other approaches.",
"1lfgd74": "Small things that help",
"1v5q2b8": "You can eat and drink a glass of water first, replace every second drink with a non-alcoholic one, choose a smaller glass or a weaker drink, and tell a friend or partner that you are cutting down. These are tips from the guidelines, not from studies that tested each tip separately. Not every tip suits everyone; you can choose one or two.",
"1no6vkw": "Sleep, and when to see a doctor",
"13j8527": "Alcohol in the evening can harm sleep quality. If during cutting down you get strong morning anxiety, sweating, shaking, nausea or vomiting, contact a doctor. If you get strong shaking, confusion, hallucinations or a seizure, it is an emergency: go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"1cfo45o": "Every small step counts. If it did not work out, it is not a failure but information about what is hard for you. You can come back here at any point.",
"1f6lw2c": "Alcohol-reduction coach: a research-based personal plan",
"9pehjg": "A few questions about your drinking, and a research-based plan for cutting down, with sources. All in the browser, nothing saved and your answers are never sent."
});
Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"1yhamz": "The coach needs JavaScript. You can read the habits guide instead. If you drink heavily every day, do not stop all at once without a doctor. Severe shaking, confusion, hallucinations or a seizure are an emergency: go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"l75emr": "Roughly how many alcoholic drinks do you have per week? (A drink = a glass of wine, a small bottle of beer or a shot of spirits, about 14 grams of alcohol)",
"1dchmvw": "On how many days a week do you drink alcohol?",
"7ukprk": "One or two days",
"1u9znih": "Three to five days",
"1rjfcqu": "Six or seven days",
"kxfi77": "I have never cut down or stopped",
"suh386": "Medical conditions and medicines: does any of these apply to you?",
"a3fxkm": "I have liver disease",
"1518ljt": "I have epilepsy, or I have ever had a seizure",
"snt2ic": "I have been hospitalised or had a detox in the past because of alcohol",
"ywkarf": "I have a strong craving to drink, or I have tried to cut down several times and could not",
"1t44i1o": "I take sedative, sleeping or anxiety medicines (for example benzodiazepines)",
"10uqiod": "I take strong pain medicines, or I use drugs",
"1rsshym": "I take antidepressants or another regular medicine",
"1449hsx": "What to do now",
"p20azm": "This coach is for adults only. If you are young, it is worth talking to your doctor or a counsellor. If something dangerous is happening right now, go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"17mq8ya": "You shared something important",
"u6spbg": "Thoughts of harming yourself are not something you have to carry alone, and you can get help even if you are not sure what exactly is going on. If you are in immediate danger now, or you feel you might harm yourself tonight: go to the nearest emergency room or call the ambulance service, or ask someone close to you to stay with you.",
"fv964k": "You can also talk to someone right now: ERAN, emotional first aid, by phone on 1201 (and by chat on eran.org.il).",
"1lsm7sm": "If you can, move away anything you could use to hurt yourself, and it is best not to drink tonight if you feel this way. Also tell your family doctor about these thoughts.",
"jfusa1": "If you are pregnant or planning a pregnancy, the UK guidance says the safest approach is not to drink at all. If you drank small amounts before you knew you were pregnant, the guidance says the risk is likely to be low. If it is hard to stop, tell your doctor or midwife. This is a normal part of the conversation and there is nothing to be ashamed of.",
"xftfrk": "If you drink a lot, do not stop all at once on your own: see a doctor or midwife soon. This is urgent, because stopping suddenly can be dangerous in pregnancy too.",
"13jj8tt": "We will not show a plan for cutting down during pregnancy.",
"191gs1q": "From what you told us, your body may be used to alcohol, or there may be a condition that makes cutting down on your own unsafe. In some people, cutting down sharply or stopping suddenly can cause shaking, sweating, confusion, hallucinations and even seizures, and the risk is higher in people who have had this before. This does not mean you did anything wrong. It means a doctor should guide the process.",
"xp9jp": "So we are not showing a plan for cutting down on your own. The best step now: book an appointment with your family doctor and tell them honestly how much you drink, and which medicines and medical conditions you have. The doctor can also explain whether there are medicines that could help; that is the doctor's decision, not the website's.",
"1ovxayt": "Mixing alcohol with sedative, sleeping or strong pain medicines, or with drugs, can be dangerous even in relatively small amounts. Do not stop a medicine or change a dose without a doctor, and ask your doctor or pharmacist whether it is safe to drink with them.",
"70itor": "If you have liver disease, epilepsy or a past seizure, or a past hospitalisation or detox because of alcohol, it is especially important that a doctor guides the reduction.",
"be4sae": "In the meantime, do not stop all at once without a doctor, even if you feel well.",
"5kz04n": "If shaking, heavy sweating or confusion appear when you are not drinking, see a doctor the same day.",
"vs189c": "Before you start: one safety rule",
"11l7lrr": "If you drink every day, or you used to feel unwell when you did not drink, do not stop all at once without a doctor, even if you feel well.",
"xmn512": "If while cutting down you get strong anxiety in the morning, sweating, shaking, nausea or vomiting: stop cutting down and see a doctor the same day.",
"9i411z": "Good to know",
"1kjwk08": "Not all the safety questions were answered, or you have not yet tried to cut down, so it is impossible to know how your body will react. If you drink a lot or every day, or shaking or sweating appear when you do not drink, talk to a doctor first.",
"i82spb": "Medicines",
"puv3q0": "If you take prescription medicines, ask your doctor or pharmacist whether it is safe to drink with them. With some medicines, mixing with alcohol can be dangerous, even if you do not drink much. Do not stop or change a medicine without talking to your doctor.",
"fry8fg": "In trials of brief conversations with people who drink at risky levels but are not dependent on alcohol, consumption fell by about 20 grams per week on average after a year (34 studies, 15,197 participants), compared with a control group. It was not tested whether an online tool works the same way. This is a modest step that shows a small change is possible. You can set a limit before going out and write down at the end of the day how much you drank. We do not know whether the writing down itself helps.",
"1en92ca": "According to the UK guidance, it is best not to regularly exceed 14 units a week, and to spread drinking evenly over three days or more. A UK unit is 8 grams, so 14 units is about 112 grams, which is about 8 drinks by the definition used here (about 14 grams per drink), not 14 drinks. In the Wood study (Lancet 2018) the risk of death was lowest at around 100 grams per week or less (about 7 drinks). Less is better.",
"609udp": "Daily drinking",
"190gyg4": "If you drink every day, do not make alcohol-free days all at once. If you want to cut down, do it gradually and with a doctor, and do not stop all at once.",
"7qhn6r": "You can eat and drink a glass of water beforehand, replace every second drink with a non-alcoholic one, choose a smaller glass or a weaker drink, and tell a friend or partner that you are cutting down. These are tips from the guidance, not from studies that tested each tip separately. Not every tip suits everyone; you can pick one or two. Alcohol in the evening can disrupt sleep, especially in the second half of the night. If you drink to relax or to sleep, tell your doctor: there are other ways.",
"1ndgmn1": "Every small step counts. If it did not work, that is not a failure but information about what is hard for you. And if it is hard, you can ask for help; that is not a sign of weakness. If you struggle to cut down again and again, it is worth talking to a doctor."
});
