"v1k4xl": "I have heart disease, or I have chest pain, strong palpitations, fainting or shortness of breath",
"14scvu5": "Age 65 and over",
"1fquscd": "At age 65 and over, losing weight is not always advisable, because part of the loss is muscle. So we do not show a weight-loss plan here. What is worth doing: eat enough, do not skimp on protein, keep up movement and strength and balance training, and check with a doctor or dietitian before trying to lose weight. How much protein suits you is worth asking a dietitian.",
"1p8hzt4": "Based on the height and weight entered, the weight is low or close to the bottom of the normal range. In this situation losing weight is not recommended, and there are also medical reasons for low weight. It is worth talking to a doctor before any change.",
"1h63o3d": "From your answers there may be a difficulty with food, and then a weight-loss plan could make it worse. So we are not showing a plan. This does not mean something is wrong with you, and help is available. It is worth contacting your family doctor and asking for a referral to a dietitian or a therapist who specialises in eating disorders.",
"inzrmk": "If your physical condition is worrying (fainting, chest pain, severe weakness), or if thoughts of harming yourself come up, go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"1jpf2nv": "When taking diabetes medicines, a change in diet or weight loss may require adjusting the treatment. Do not change it on your own. It is worth consulting your treating doctor first.",
"ou0q13": "Bariatric surgery or a weight-loss medicine",
"1lrp6uq": "After bariatric surgery, or during treatment with a weight-loss medicine, diet and weight are managed with the treating team. It is worth consulting them rather than relying on a general plan.",
"1a3x2yy": "Heart disease or symptoms",
"4ocoad": "Heart disease, or chest pain, strong palpitations, fainting or shortness of breath, are a reason to see a doctor before changing diet or activity. If the symptoms are happening now, go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"kjqeom": "No age entered",
"103tmqi": "No age was entered. This coach is for ages 18 to 64. If you are under 18 or 65 and over, the plan here is not suitable.",
"4c7qor": "Worth checking the data",
"w574vb": "The height and weight entered give a very high BMI. Please make sure you entered centimetres and kilograms (not inches or pounds).",
"tuqq00": "You can start small. In studies, losing 3% to 5% of body weight, if kept off, is linked to improvements in blood sugar and triglycerides, and losing about 5% also in blood pressure. This is true mainly for people with excess weight or risk factors. The loss usually starts fast and levels off after about half a year, which is normal and not a failure.",
"d7qjyi": "In the PREDIMED trial, people aged 55 to 80 at high cardiovascular risk who ate a Mediterranean diet with extra-virgin olive oil or nuts had fewer heart attacks and strokes. The absolute difference was small. The trial was not designed to lower weight, so we do not promise weight loss, but this is an eating pattern with evidence for heart health.",
"10ebs3o": "Eating you can keep up, without focusing on weight",
"ez3qkl": "No single diet has been shown to win. What matters is a pattern that suits you and that you can keep up over time, without bans and without counting.",
"c8d77q": "In the PREDIMED trial, people aged 55 to 80 at high cardiovascular risk who ate a Mediterranean diet with extra-virgin olive oil or nuts had fewer heart attacks and strokes. The absolute difference was small. This is an eating pattern with evidence for heart health, and you can start from it without changing everything.",
"6iz12s": "Guidelines recommend at least 150 minutes a week of moderate activity, such as brisk walking. You can start with 10 minutes and add.",
"1688d8c": "When people lose weight, part of the loss is muscle. In one trial of older adults with obesity who lost about 9% of body weight, those who added strength training lost less lean mass (2% to 3% versus 5% with aerobic exercise alone). It is not known whether this also applies to younger people. For how much protein suits you, it is worth consulting a dietitian.",
"1u65614": "In cohort studies, people who usually drank 1 to 2 sugary drinks a day developed type 2 diabetes 26% more often than people who drank almost none. This is a statistical link from observational studies, not proof that the drinks caused it. Swapping for water, soda water or unsweetened tea is one of the easiest steps to start with.",
"h1f5fa": "In a small hospital-based trial of 20 adults, in which each person ate under both conditions in turn, on a diet high in ultra-processed food people ate about 500 more calories a day and gained about 0.9 kg in two weeks (and on the unprocessed diet they lost about 0.9 kg). Most other evidence is low-quality observational research, and it is not yet proven that the processing itself is the cause. You can notice how much of your food is ready-made, packaged and easy to eat a lot of.",
"v9rgce": "Losing the weight is the easier half. Guidelines recommend a maintenance programme of at least a year, with regular monitoring and higher activity. If you tried before and the weight came back, this is a well-known pattern and not a sign that something is wrong with you.",
"19bi394": "Very-low-calorie diets (under 800 calories a day) are suitable only in limited cases and in a medical setting with monitoring, according to AHA guidance. Do not try this on your own.",
"1mqwbf7": "There are also medicines for obesity. They are prescribed only by a doctor, and they have side effects and contraindications. According to the 2013 AHA/ACC/TOS guideline (expert opinion, and knowledge may have moved on since), a medicine can be considered as an addition to lifestyle change when BMI is 30 or more, or 27 or more with a related medical condition. This is not a personal recommendation, and we do not recommend any medicine or dose. If relevant, also ask your doctor what happens after stopping.",
"1o9hug2": "This coach is general and does not replace personal advice. If you have a medical condition, take medicines or are unsure, it is worth consulting a doctor or dietitian before making a significant change to your diet. If chest pain, fainting or fast weight loss without a reason appear, see a doctor."
