Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"13n440r": "In a preliminary check we ran on US NHANES data (about 23,500 adults, 10-year follow-up) the model separated high-risk from low-risk people well, but on average predicted about 15 to 20 percent more deaths than were observed. We therefore added a calibration: the combined risk ratio is multiplied by 0.75, gradually according to the number of factors entered (no calibration up to two factors, full calibration from eight factors). The calibration was estimated on the same data, so it is not an independent check; it has not been reviewed by an actuary and was not done on an Israeli population. It raises the estimate by roughly 0.5 to 1 year for someone who entered four factors, and by about 2 years for someone who entered many, so the absolute estimate is less certain than the comparison between habits."
});

Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"1v4dw2q": "The factors you entered are so favourable that the model reaches its lower risk limit, so changes in lab values do not change the estimate. This does not mean the tests are unimportant."
});

Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"g28ii9": "Statistical details",
"s0k5c1": "Updated November 2026",
"1f7j3pr": "Sleep is not wasted time. The good news: chronic sleep problems have an effective treatment without medication.",
"17evhfa": "first line",
"16u10r3": "When falling asleep is hard: in Trauer's meta-analysis (2015)[[20 randomised trials, 1,162 participants with chronic insomnia]], cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT-I) included at least three of these components: cognitive therapy, stimulus control (the bed is used only for sleep), sleep restriction, sleep hygiene and relaxation. It shortened the time to fall asleep by about 19 minutes[[95% CI: 14–24]], reduced night-time wakefulness by about 26 minutes, and raised sleep efficiency by about 10 percentage points. Total sleep time barely changed (about 8 minutes, not significant). In other words, the treatment improves the quality of sleep, not necessarily its length.",
"pvn3av": "Surroundings and evening habits: according to advice from the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), aim for a quiet, dark bedroom at a comfortable temperature, with no screens or phone by the bed, no heavy meal or alcohol in the hours before sleep, and physical activity during the day. In the morning, it is worth getting some daylight. These are general sleep hygiene tips, not the result of a particular trial.",
"j5gkyo": "Someone who sleeps less than 7 hours needs to move their bedtime earlier in practice, not just wish to sleep more. In Tasali's trial (2022), 80 adults aged 21–40 with overweight, who slept under 6.5 hours a night, took part. Half of them received one individual sleep-hygiene counselling session, aimed at reaching 8.5 hours in bed. They slept about 1.2 hours more per night on average[[95% CI: 1.0–1.4]] than the group that changed nothing, and ate about 270 fewer calories a day[[95% CI: 147–393]], without being told to change their diet.",
"cz9dn": "Obstructive sleep apnoea is repeated pauses in breathing during the night. In the meta-analysis by Fu (2017)[[27 cohort studies; the comparison by severity was based on 11 studies]], severe apnoea was linked to about 2.1 times the risk of death from any cause compared with people without apnoea[[HR 2.13, 95% confidence interval: 1.68–2.68]]. The authors note publication bias and only partial adjustment for risk factors in some studies. For mild and moderate apnoea the link was not statistically significant[[1.19 for mild, 1.28 for moderate]].",
"13mh2ch": "In the meta-analysis by Benjafield (2025)[[30 studies, ten of them randomised trials]], positive airway pressure treatment (PAP, including CPAP) was linked to 37% lower all-cause mortality compared with no treatment or little use[[HR 0.63, 95% confidence interval: 0.56–0.72]]. In the randomised trials alone there was no statistically significant difference[[0.87, 95% confidence interval: 0.65–1.16]]. The overall significance came from non-randomised studies, so part of the difference may be due to other differences between people who are treated and people who are not. A trend toward greater benefit with more use was seen, but the difference was not significant. The authors describe this as a possible effect, and the study was funded by ResMed, a maker of PAP devices.",
"84i20q": "Melatonin: in the meta-analysis by Ferracioli-Oda (2013)[[19 trials, 1,683 participants with primary sleep disorders]], melatonin shortened the time to fall asleep by about 7 minutes compared with placebo[[95% CI: 4.4–9.8]] and lengthened total sleep time by about 8 minutes[[95% CI: 1.7–14.8]]. The authors call the effect modest, and note that it did not fade with continued use.",
"1tsh4j3": "Magnesium: in a review by Mah (2021)[[three trials, 151 older adults with insomnia]], sleep onset time was shortened by about 17 minutes compared with placebo[[95% confidence interval: 7.4–27.3]], with no significant improvement in total sleep time. All the trials were at moderate to high risk of bias, and the evidence was of low to very low quality, so the authors conclude that the quality of the literature is too low for an informed recommendation. They add that because magnesium is cheap and widely available, the evidence may support its use for insomnia symptoms.",
"s0fqya": "Chamomile, the natural source of apigenin: in a meta-analysis of 10 trials (2024)[[772 participants]], the PSQI score fell by 1.88 points[[95% CI: 0.31–3.46, with high heterogeneity]], but sleep duration and sleep efficiency did not improve.",
"11gksg4": "Supplements with no controlled trials for sleep: apigenin alone and phosphatidylserine have not been tested in a randomised controlled trial in adults (the only trial on apigenin tested a mixture of several ingredients). For myo-inositol there is one trial in pregnant women (2022) and none in the general population. That means there is no evidence yet, not that they do not work. There is also no known research on a combination of all these supplements together.",
"2j6hj0": "Legumes and nuts: according to the World Health Organization, a healthy diet includes legumes (lentils, chickpeas, beans), nuts and whole grains. Legumes are a source of protein and fibre, and are worth including two or three times a week.",
"10ibdmz": "Simple ways to start: close the kitchen after dinner, or try a 12-hour eating window (for example 8:00 to 20:00) and see how you feel. Fasting does not cancel out what you eat inside the window, so keep the quality of the food high there too. If you take medication, especially for diabetes or blood pressure, talk to a doctor first.",
"3hea6g": "Check the packaging: in Israel, a red label marks a product high in sugar (or in sodium or saturated fat). It is worth looking for it on yogurts, breakfast cereals and snacks.",
"1fah5lf": "150 minutes a week is about 30 minutes five times a week, and a short walk after a meal can count toward it.",
"d8vf5s": "A simple way to get there: a brisk 30-minute walk, five days a week, adds up to 150 minutes. To see where you are starting from, count your steps for a week and add 1,000 steps a day to the average you get. If you sit for work, get up for a short movement break every hour. Strength training at home can use just your body weight: squats, push-ups and planks.",
"1tv0db0": "After 65 there is also the matter of balance: at least a third of people this age fall every year. In a Cochrane review of 108 randomised trials with 23,407 participants (Sherrington 2019), exercise reduced the rate of falls by 23%[[RaR 0.77, 95% CI 0.71–0.83]]. That is why the World Health Organization guidelines for people aged 65 and over include varied activity that emphasises balance and strength, on at least three days a week.",
"5kja85": "A conversation with someone close is also a tool: social connection is included in the calculator, and you can read about it in the \"Social ties\" guide. It is also worth saving the ERAN number, 1201, in your phone in advance, in case you or someone close needs it.",
"12aupp": "This may be the most surprising finding in the field: social connections are a health factor of a strength similar to well-known risk factors such as smoking and physical activity.",
"z2n0rr": "What to do in practice: the studies looked at relationships, not at particular exercises, but the direction is clear: regular contact with people, taking part in a group, and depth in relationships. You can start with a regular conversation with one person (a fixed day and time, even 15 minutes), a group activity such as walking, a choir, volunteering or a class, and inviting someone to a meal for no particular reason.",
"69mj1b": "How to start: set a date to quit smoking and tell people close to you, and contact your health fund about a quit-smoking workshop and the medications included in the health basket. For alcohol: count your drinks each week, stay under 6 glasses of wine, and set a few fixed days a week without alcohol.",
"sqm3gl": "A man or a woman with a BMI of 32 is in grade 1 obesity, so the grade 1 ratio for their sex applies, as given in \"What the data say\". These are group hazard ratios and not a personal forecast, and the calculator combines them with age, smoking, blood tests and the other factors.",
"1a59w1p": "In a pooled study by Cerhan (Mayo Clin Proc 2014), every additional 5 cm of waist circumference was linked to an increase of about 7% to 9% in mortality risk. The figure is taken from the study abstract. That is why the calculator also asks about waist circumference: it helps to tell apart people with the same BMI.",
"1b743vx": "A realistic target: a small change you can keep up over time is better than an \"ideal\" target that is hard to meet. You can check in the calculator how many years such a change is worth.",
"1mbbom6": "mortality for each year that the PhenoAge is higher[[confidence interval 1.08–1.10]]",
"19au3w8": "In Liu's study (PLoS Med 2018), each year of PhenoAge above age was linked to a hazard ratio of 1.09[[confidence interval 1.08–1.10]] for all-cause mortality. In predicting 10-year mortality, the AUC of PhenoAge alone was 0.879, versus 0.855 for age alone. So it adds to age, but modestly.",
"oli7l3": "Levine's formula uses age and these nine values from a routine blood test. The weights were derived from a survival model on American data, and the whole formula has been published, including its 2019 correction.",
"bvuw8k": "The calculator computes biological age only when all nine values from a 12-month window are present. You can upload a blood-test file and the values are filled in locally in your browser. The difference from actual age enters as one factor, and is capped (see \"Why the difference is limited\"). When it exists, kidney function and CRP are not counted separately, so that they are not counted twice.",
"8318e8": "There are other measures called \"biological age\", including epigenetic clocks based on chemical marks on DNA. They are not included in the calculator. PhenoAge was chosen because it is calculated from blood tests that most people already take, and its formula has been published in full.",
"1al9top": "A population's life expectancy does not take into account your habits, tests and family history. The CBS also publishes further breakdowns, for example by population group and region, and they are not included here. To see what the number is for you, the calculator starts from the life table for your age and sex and adjusts it to habits and tests.",
"wzw3a1": "In the VITAL trial, vitamin D did not change overall mortality[[HR 0.99]]",
"1qt37fa": "In Cochrane, omega-3 barely changed overall mortality[[RR 0.97]]",
"1t70qe8": "In the VITAL trial (Manson 2019), 25,871 participants received vitamin D or placebo for a median of 5.3 years. No difference was found in cancer[[HR 0.96, 95% CI: 0.88–1.06]], in cardiovascular events[[HR 0.97, 0.85–1.12]], or in death from any cause[[HR 0.99, 0.87–1.12]]. Someone with a deficiency found in a blood test should decide on a supplement together with their doctor.",
"1yp82gz": "In the Cochrane review (Abdelhamid 2020), 86 trials with 162,796 participants, omega-3 supplements barely changed overall mortality[[RR 0.97, 95% CI: 0.93–1.01, high certainty]]. There was a small reduction in death from heart disease and in coronary events[[RR 0.91 for coronary events]], but certainty was low to moderate: about 167 people would need to be treated to prevent one coronary event.",
"12kcu3f": "In VITAL (25,871 participants) there was no difference in overall mortality[[HR 0.99]].",
"1wrlydw": "In Bjelakovic's meta-analysis (2007)[[47 high-quality trials, 180,938 participants]], antioxidant supplements slightly raised mortality[[RR 1.05, 95% CI: 1.02–1.08]]. Beta-carotene, vitamin A and vitamin E raised mortality, alone or combined[[RR 1.07 for beta-carotene, 1.16 for vitamin A, 1.04 for vitamin E]]. No effect on mortality was found for vitamin C or selenium. This is a 2007 study.",
"1byucmu": "B12 is a case where testing and a supplement are sometimes justified. In the DPPOS trial (Aroda 2016), among people who took metformin for years, low B12 (up to 203 pg/mL) was more common than in the placebo group after 5 years (4.3% vs 2.3%), and low or borderline B12 was even more common (19.1% vs 9.5%). Each year of treatment added risk[[OR 1.13 per year]]. The researchers recommended considering routine B12 testing for people on metformin. If a low value is found, the doctor will decide on treatment and dose.",
"lr68hs": "In Chilibeck's meta-analysis (2017)[[22 trials, 721 participants aged 57–70 on average]], creatine supplementation alongside resistance training increased lean muscle mass by 1.37 kg[[95% CI: 0.97–1.76]] and slightly increased strength. This is a measure of muscle, not of mortality: the studies did not test whether it extends life. The training itself is what matters most.",
"1vst8w2": "What to check about a supplement: a claim such as \"extends life\" needs research in humans that looked at disease or mortality, not only blood markers or experiments in mice. And what is better to do first: invest in habits with evidence, such as sleep, movement and nutrition (see the guides).",
"qjg8c9": "What to do in practice: a routine blood test should include glucose, HbA1c, lipids and kidney function. You can measure blood pressure at home for three days, morning and evening, and write it down. Waist circumference is measured at the level of the navel, at the end of a normal breath out. You can enter the values in the calculator, or upload the test file: the values are read in the browser and the file is not sent anywhere.",
"fyyvzr": "Every guide is based on studies from medical journals and on guidelines from bodies such as the World Health Organization. Every number has a link to its source, and the numbers were checked against the study's abstract. When a study only shows a link and does not prove a cause (an observational study), it says so.",
"nw4ez3": "The starting point is the World Health Organization's mortality table by country, age and sex. Each risk factor, for example smoking, is turned into a hazard ratio: a number from a large study that says by how much the factor multiplies or reduces the risk of dying at any age. The effect weakens in older age. Factors that are related to each other are counted with a smaller weight, so that the same thing is not counted twice. The result is shown as a range, because nobody can know a date.",
"1gfo8iw": "The model combines findings from separate studies, and there is no single study behind it that was tested against real data. It is not a validated model.",
"tiqp5w": "Factors that are related to each other, such as weight, sugar and blood pressure, partly affect the same thing. How the calculator handles this is an approximation, and it can overstate or understate, especially for someone with many risk factors.",
"1756ta8": "The mortality table reflects today's mortality, not future improvements in medicine. So the estimate for young people is probably conservative, meaning too low.",
"10nrcz2": "The range shown reflects how much people with the same profile can differ from one another. It does not show the uncertainty of the model itself.",
"h8px1s": "Most studies are observational: they show a link, and it is not always possible to know how much of it is cause and effect. So \"how many years you will gain\" is an estimate, not a promise.",
"1ek6la8": "Many of the studies were carried out in Western countries. The model does not know about rare diseases, genetics or acute, meaning sudden, risks.",
"1kyczvq": "Every week PubMed, the database of medical articles, is scanned for new studies on mortality and life expectancy: meta-analyses (a summary of several studies), controlled trials and large follow-up studies. Every study found is checked against its abstract and against the numbers already on the site. Only a strong study that changes the picture updates a number in the calculator or a guide.",
"yocbfh": "Your answers are not sent anywhere. The calculation and the reading of blood-test files and watch files all happen in your browser. If you choose to remember your data, it is stored only in this browser, and you can delete it at any moment with the \"Delete my data\" button at the bottom of the page.",
"10aacvv": "You can check for yourself: once the page has loaded, disconnect from the internet or switch to airplane mode, and the calculator keeps working, even with a blood-test file.",
"11r2w4": "Each risk factor is turned into a hazard ratio (HR) from a study: a number that says by how much the factor multiplies or reduces the risk of dying at any age. The effect weakens in older age.",
"1qgiq3b": "Taken from earlier documentation and not yet re-checked. Marked openly.",
"wlrx83": "The values in the table are hazard ratios (HR) for death from any cause, unless stated otherwise. A ratio above 1 means a higher risk, and below 1 a lower risk. For some studies the model uses only some of the numbers, and the table shows the main ones.",
"no8smd": "With coffee: change the habit (tea, a different place, a different time), because coffee is a regular trigger for the urge to smoke.",
"16buvya": "You can start small. In studies, losing 3% to 5% of body weight, if kept off, is linked to improvements in blood sugar and blood fats (triglycerides), and losing about 5% also in blood pressure. This is true mainly for people with excess weight or risk factors. The pace of loss differs from person to person. For some it starts fast and levels off after about half a year, and that too is normal and not a failure.",
"1cip8hi": "In follow-up studies, people who usually drank 1 to 2 sugary drinks a day developed type 2 diabetes 26% more often than people who drank almost none. This is a statistical link from observational studies, not proof that the drinks caused it. Swapping for water, soda water or unsweetened tea is one of the easiest steps to start with.",
"1nhetnq": "In trials of brief conversations with people who drink at risky levels but are not dependent on alcohol, consumption fell by about 20 grams per week on average after a year[[34 studies, 15,197 participants]], compared with a group that did not have such a conversation. It was not tested whether an online tool works the same way. This is a modest step that shows a small change is possible. You can set a limit before going out and write down at the end of the day how much you drank. We do not know whether the writing down itself helps.",
"156c56e": "<b>Marital status:</b> Wang Y. et al. Sex differences in the association between marital status and the risk of cardiovascular, cancer, and all-cause mortality. <i>Glob Health Res Policy</i> 2020;5:4. <span class=\"hrnote\">In the model: no partner: 1.46 in men, 1.22 in women</span>",
"1ykw59f": "<b>Smoking:</b> Jha P. et al. 21st-Century Hazards of Smoking and Benefits of Cessation in the United States. <i>NEJM</i> 2013;368:341–50. <span class=\"hrnote\">In the model: current smoker: 2.8 in men, 3.0 in women. Quit at 25–34: 1.0, 35–44: 1.2, 45–54: 1.5, 55–64: 1.7; over 65: 1.9 (assumption)</span>",
"1b3yb7e": "Doctor visit sheet",
"1tl52q0": "Among Ashkenazi Jews, about 1 in 40 carries a BRCA1/2 mutation, which raises the risk of breast, ovarian and prostate cancer. You can ask your doctor about genetic testing.",
"1kq87vz": "A family history of breast, ovarian or colon cancer is not part of the number, but it is a good reason to ask a doctor about early screening and genetic counseling.",
"1wt3ict": ") is below 60. A single result is not a diagnosis, but it is worth showing to your doctor.",
"1aevlbm": "The combination you entered reaches the upper limit of the model, so the estimate is less certain. It is worth discussing with a doctor.",
"10zfgdw": "You indicated cancer, chronic lung disease or another serious illness. These are not included in the number: their effect depends heavily on the type, stage and treatment, and the model is built on the general population. So the estimate here is probably too optimistic for you, and your treating doctor is the right person to ask.",
"gf18n5": "High cholesterol can be managed with diet and treatment. Worth talking to your doctor.",
"1tuw2wb": "High blood pressure can be treated effectively. Worth measuring again and talking to your doctor.",
"pbvd3r": "<div class=\"simrow\"><label for=\"s_quit\">Smoking</label><select id=\"s_quit\" data-sim=\"quit\"><option value=\"\">Keep smoking</option><option value=\"1\">Quit smoking now</option></select></div>",
"1haw1hp": "<p class=\"small muted\">The data were entered by the user and have not been verified. This is not a medical diagnosis, only a sheet to prepare the conversation. It is created in your browser only.</p>",
"1oiuky0": "<h2>Preparation sheet for my doctor visit</h2>",
"d6uib9": "<div class=\"tests\"><h3>Tests worth raising with your doctor</h3><p class=\"small muted\">Based on your age and what you entered. Guidelines vary between countries; this is a list for a conversation with your doctor, not a referral.</p>",
"nfxfka": "Low-dose CT for lung cancer: based on the smoking and age you entered (ages 50–80, current smoker or quit within the last 15 years). The guidelines are for people who smoked heavily over many years, and the amount smoked is not asked here, so it is worth checking with your doctor whether it applies to you.",
"yhc2j": "<p class=\"small muted\">Chest pain, dizziness or unusual shortness of breath during exertion are a sign to stop and see a doctor. This is general information, not medical advice.</p>",
"lbq14r": "<p class=\"small\">You have a chronic condition (diabetes or blood pressure), so it is worth asking your doctor which intensity suits you before you step it up.</p>",
"yg6z5o": "<p class=\"small muted\">Schematic illustrations, not instructions for performing the exercises. If something is unclear or hurts, ask a trainer or physiotherapist.</p>",
"7uiaf3": "<div class=\"planref\"><b>First of all: worth talking to a doctor</b><ul>",
"1724zuc": "Book an appointment with your doctor to discuss the values. Meanwhile, swap animal fats for olive oil and nuts.",
"qle67d": "Measure your blood pressure at home for a few days, write it down and show your doctor.",
"260s3m": "Set a quit date in the next two weeks, and contact your doctor or a quitline. Medication and support greatly improve the chances of success.",
"r9zoka": "There is protein in your urine (above 30 mg/g). Repeat the test and show it to your doctor.",
"1c65sw1": ") is below 60. Repeat the test and show it to your doctor.",
"kwq6x9": "The cholesterol value you entered is very high and warrants a check for familial high cholesterol. It's worth seeing your doctor.",
"15xj8gs": "The blood sugar value you entered is in the diabetes range. A diagnosis needs a repeat test, so it's worth seeing your doctor.",
"qczwgz": ") is in the high blood pressure range. Measure it again at home over a few days and show the results to your doctor.",
"1mkb7m9": ") is very high. Please see a doctor soon.",
"1uyndzo": "<p class=\"small muted\">These values are not part of the life expectancy calculation. The range is the one printed on the document itself, and the reading is automatic and can be wrong; a value out of range is a reason to ask your doctor, not a diagnosis.</p>",
"1shad4h": ">Deceased</option></select>",
"1rzmlqa": "How would you describe your usual walking pace?",
"8r49r7": "Current smoker",
"12h9l6o": "How would you rate your health overall?",
"3cp8wq": "No partner",
"1ie66lf": "Married or in a steady relationship",
"2l8jnh": "It describes averages of large groups of people, not the future of any one person. It is not a medical diagnosis, not medical advice and not a substitute for a doctor.",
"1wr2a34": "Grandparents' average: ",
"y2zmsg": "Married or partnered",
"oe5w37": "A CRP above 10 mg/L usually reflects a passing inflammation or infection, not long-term risk. The value was capped at 10 in the calculation; it is better to enter a result from a test taken while you were well.",
"13j64u": "The values you entered (fasting glucose of 126 or more, or HbA1c of 6.5% or more) are in the range defined as diabetes. If you haven't been diagnosed, it is worth seeing a doctor.",
"q2m2dl": "Your blood sugar values are in the \"prediabetes\" range. The ERFC study found about 10% higher risk per 18 mg/dL above 100, and this is included in the calculation (if glucose was entered). Worth following up with your doctor.",
"lpxurt": "This is not a diagnosis or medical or nutritional advice. We do not give calorie targets, medications or doses. When in doubt, ask a doctor or dietitian.",
"1wp47sy": "The coach needs JavaScript. You can read the habits guide instead. If you drink heavily every day, do not stop all at once without a doctor. Severe shaking, confusion, hallucinations or a seizure are an emergency: go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"17gky5d": "This is not a diagnosis or medical advice. We do not give withdrawal instructions, medications or doses. Anyone who drinks heavily is referred to a doctor. When in doubt, ask a doctor.",
"1x8gtub": "This is not a diagnosis or medical advice. We do not recommend a specific prescription drug or doses. When in doubt, ask a doctor.",
"1qjqiz9": "This is not a diagnosis or medical advice. We do not recommend prescription drugs or doses. When in doubt, ask a doctor.",
"sgl153": "It is not a diagnosis and not medical advice. The studies describe averages of large groups, not the future of any one person. For any question about your health, talk to your doctor.",
"vo8fxg": "General research-based information, not medical advice and not a substitute for a doctor. Before a significant change, especially with an illness or medication, talk to a professional.",
"1afn95v": "Non-smoker",
"aomsx": "Target LDL cholesterol values depend on personal risk, so they are best set with a doctor. Lp(a) is usually tested once in a lifetime, because it is mainly determined by genetics.",
"l8x5qs": "A high number is a reason to check with a doctor and to improve sleep, activity and weight.",
"adi318": "If biological age is higher than actual age, it is worth checking which value is out of range and discussing it with a doctor. The values themselves are linked to familiar habits such as weight, sugar, activity and sleep, and the guides on the site cover each of them. A single number is a starting point, not a verdict, and a trend across several tests may be more informative.",
"1785dep": "is a reason to see a doctor.",
"oyyh0z": "It is worth talking to a doctor when the BMI is 30 or more, when weight rises or falls quickly for no clear reason, or when excess weight comes together with high blood pressure or high blood sugar. A doctor can assess the whole picture and suggest suitable treatment.",
"rxhqqn": "When to talk to a doctor",
"14c86ir": "If you live with a partner, you're not lonely",
"zcmohr": "If stress lasts for weeks or affects your sleep or functioning, talk to your doctor or a mental health professional. In the US you can call or text 988; in the UK, Samaritans are on 116 123. In an emergency, call your local emergency number (911 in the US, 999 in the UK, 112 in Europe).",
"1h3dkau": "If you enjoy it, there's no reason to give it up. Drink water and don't go in after alcohol. Anyone with unstable heart disease, low blood pressure, or who is pregnant should talk to a doctor first.",
"9witmk": "Fasting is not for everyone. People on diabetes medication (especially insulin), pregnant or breastfeeding women, and anyone with a history of eating disorders should consult a doctor first.",
"aycbhv": "Bottom line: an interesting method with good early results on markers, but it hasn't been shown to extend life. The same caveats as for fasting apply here, and anyone on medication should talk to a doctor first.",
"74mdka": "If a particular supplement helps you, there is no conclusion here that you should stop. It is only worth checking with a doctor or pharmacist, especially if you take regular medication, are pregnant, or the sleep problem is new and sudden.",
"1518rbh": "Benzodiazepines: in 2020 the FDA updated the boxed warning for the whole class: risk of misuse, addiction, physical dependence and withdrawal reactions. Combining them with opioids or alcohol raises the risk of overdose and death. They should not be stopped all at once but tapered gradually with a doctor, because withdrawal can include seizures.",
"18itmqv": "Zolpidem, eszopiclone and zaleplon: in 2019 the FDA added a boxed warning about complex sleep behaviors, such as sleepwalking or sleep-driving, that caused serious injuries and deaths. The events happened even at the lowest recommended dose and after a single dose, and the person usually does not remember them. Anyone who notices such behavior should stop the medicine and contact a doctor.",
"rkljld": "Prescription sleep medicines suit some people, as a decision made with a doctor. These are official warnings from the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA), not a call to avoid them in every case.",
"12hterw": "Sleep apnea is not included in the calculation. Loud snoring, breathing pauses that someone has noticed, or severe tiredness during the day are a reason to see a family doctor, who can refer for a sleep study.",
"1os78fk": "According to the American College of Physicians guideline (2016), the first treatment for chronic insomnia is cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT-I), before medication. It is available face to face, in a group, through an app or from a book. Loud snoring, pauses in breathing or severe daytime tiredness are a reason to see your doctor.",
"42961w": "Every small step counts. If it did not work, that is not a failure but information about what is hard for you. And if it is hard, you can ask for help; that is not a sign of weakness. If you struggle to cut down again and again, it is worth talking to a doctor.",
"svw4zc": "You can eat and drink a glass of water beforehand, replace every second drink with a non-alcoholic one, choose a smaller glass or a weaker drink, and tell a friend or partner that you are cutting down. These are tips from the guidance, not from studies that tested each tip separately. Not every tip suits everyone; you can pick one or two. Alcohol in the evening can disrupt sleep, especially in the second half of the night. If you drink to relax or to sleep, tell your doctor: there are other ways.",
"peprt7": "If you drink every day, do not make alcohol-free days all at once. If you want to cut down, do it gradually and with a doctor, and do not stop all at once.",
"1cp01cn": "If you take prescription medicines, ask your doctor or pharmacist whether it is safe to drink with them. With some medicines, mixing with alcohol can be dangerous, even if you do not drink much. Do not stop or change a medicine without talking to your doctor.",
"1bdg2sv": "Not all the safety questions were answered, or you have not yet tried to cut down, so it is impossible to know how your body will react. If you drink a lot or every day, or shaking or sweating appear when you do not drink, talk to a doctor first.",
"1dtiibd": "If while cutting down you get strong anxiety in the morning, sweating, shaking, nausea or vomiting: stop cutting down and see a doctor the same day.",
"1bcfnb2": "If you drink every day, or you used to feel unwell when you did not drink, do not stop all at once without a doctor, even if you feel well.",
"vi4x9m": "If shaking, heavy sweating or confusion appear when you are not drinking, see a doctor the same day.",
"1bvexwd": "In the meantime, do not stop all at once without a doctor, even if you feel well.",
"10c4yz6": "If you have liver disease, epilepsy or a past seizure, or a past hospitalisation or detox because of alcohol, it is especially important that a doctor guides the reduction.",
"1jhhu8": "Mixing alcohol with sedative, sleeping or strong pain medicines, or with drugs, can be dangerous even in relatively small amounts. Do not stop a medicine or change a dose without a doctor, and ask your doctor or pharmacist whether it is safe to drink with them.",
"ihsvcv": "So we are not showing a plan for cutting down on your own. The best step now: book an appointment with your family doctor and tell them honestly how much you drink, and which medicines and medical conditions you have. The doctor can also explain whether there are medicines that could help; that is the doctor's decision, not the website's.",
"jv8545": "From what you told us, your body may be used to alcohol, or there may be a condition that makes cutting down on your own unsafe. In some people, cutting down sharply or stopping suddenly can cause shaking, sweating, confusion, hallucinations and even seizures, and the risk is higher in people who have had this before. This does not mean you did anything wrong. It means a doctor should guide the process.",
"1erlzrg": "Before cutting down: it is worth talking to a doctor",
"18if3dr": "If you drink a lot, do not stop all at once on your own: see a doctor or midwife soon. This is urgent, because stopping suddenly can be dangerous in pregnancy too.",
"cfrcd4": "If you are pregnant or planning a pregnancy, the UK guidance says the safest approach is not to drink at all. If you drank small amounts before you knew you were pregnant, the guidance says the risk is likely to be low. If it is hard to stop, tell your doctor or midwife. This is a normal part of the conversation and there is nothing to be ashamed of.",
"vvrvfd": "If you can, move away anything you could use to hurt yourself, and it is best not to drink tonight if you feel this way. Also tell your family doctor about these thoughts.",
"1jqfijl": "This coach is for adults only. If you are young, it is worth talking to your doctor or a counsellor. If something dangerous is happening right now, go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"ndvau8": "Based on your answers. This is a tool for cutting down, not a withdrawal programme. It is general information, not a diagnosis and not a substitute for a doctor.",
"1ji32rp": "I take antidepressants or another regular medicine",
"10wq26z": "I take strong pain medicines, or I use drugs",
"1ua4l1e": "I take sedative, sleeping or anxiety medicines (for example benzodiazepines)",
"taghao": "This coach is general and does not replace personal advice. If you have a medical condition, take medicines or are unsure, it is worth consulting a doctor or dietitian before making a significant change to your diet. If chest pain, fainting or fast weight loss without a reason appear, see a doctor.",
"f2xmad": "There are also medicines for obesity. They are prescribed only by a doctor, and they have side effects and contraindications. According to the 2013 AHA/ACC/TOS guideline (expert opinion, and knowledge may have moved on since), a medicine can be considered as an addition to lifestyle change when BMI is 30 or more, or 27 or more with a related medical condition. This is not a personal recommendation, and we do not recommend any medicine or dose. If relevant, also ask your doctor what happens after stopping.",
"1akaiip": "When people lose weight, part of the loss is muscle. In one trial of older people with obesity who lost about 9% of body weight, those who added strength training lost less lean mass (2% to 3% versus 5% with aerobic exercise alone). It is not known whether this also applies to younger people. For how much protein suits you, it is worth consulting a dietitian.",
"zi0n1x": "At a higher weight, professional advice may help, including structured programmes with support and other options. You can use the suggestions here in addition to a conversation with a doctor, not instead of one.",
"1p3amk1": "A significant change in diet or weight loss may affect some regular medicines. It is worth talking to a doctor or pharmacist before changing anything.",
"1ool55g": "According to NIDDK, losing weight very quickly may raise the chance of gallbladder problems, so a slower pace is better. It is worth talking to a doctor before starting.",
"195o43x": "Based on the height and weight entered, your weight is close to the bottom of the normal range. The plan here is not meant to lower weight. If you are losing weight without trying, have a low appetite, or have an illness, it is worth talking to a doctor.",
"13s8m4h": "Height and weight were not entered, so it is not possible to check whether weight loss suits you. If your weight is low or close to the bottom of the normal range, losing weight is not recommended. It is worth talking to a doctor.",
"10g321m": "Based on your answers. This is general research-based information, not a diagnosis and not a substitute for a doctor or dietitian.",
"1ag3exk": "Heart disease, or chest pain, strong palpitations, fainting or shortness of breath, are a reason to see a doctor before changing diet or activity. If the symptoms are happening now, go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"p46cqg": "With kidney disease there are dietary limits, mainly around protein, that need individual adjustment. It is worth consulting a doctor or a kidney dietitian before changing anything.",
"5yqxtx": "During pregnancy and breastfeeding, tailored recommendations are needed. We do not show a weight-loss plan here. It is worth consulting a doctor or a dietitian.",
"uaklwz": "When taking diabetes medicines, a change in diet or weight loss may require adjusting the treatment. Do not change it on your own. It is worth consulting your treating doctor first.",
"1aimb7a": "From your answers there may be a difficulty with food, and then a weight-loss plan could make it worse. So we are not showing a plan. This does not mean something is wrong with you, and help is available. It is worth contacting your family doctor and asking for a referral to a dietitian or a therapist who specialises in eating disorders.",
"15zqpin": "Based on the height and weight entered, the weight is low or close to the bottom of the normal range. In this situation losing weight is not recommended, and there are also medical reasons for low weight. It is worth talking to a doctor before any change.",
"1im69aq": "At age 65 and over, losing weight is not always advisable, because part of the loss is muscle. So we do not show a weight-loss plan here. What is worth doing: eat enough, do not skimp on protein, keep up movement and strength and balance training, and check with a doctor or dietitian before trying to lose weight. How much protein suits you is worth asking a dietitian.",
"8gane4": "This coach is for adults only. For younger people it is worth consulting a paediatrician or a dietitian.",
"afy114": "I take other regular medicines (for example blood thinners, blood pressure medicines or psychiatric medicines)",
"tsn3e1": "I have had bariatric surgery, or I take a medicine for weight loss",
"f74ebv": "I have diabetes and take diabetes medicines (insulin or tablets)",
"1rllb64": "I strongly avoid food considered unhealthy or not clean, and I feel anxious or guilty when I eat it",
"106nmh4": "I count calories or weigh myself compulsively, or thoughts about food and weight take up most of my day",
"fz3jxc": "I make myself vomit, or use diet pills, laxatives or excessive exercise to make up for eating",
"6hppo6": "I have binge episodes in which I feel out of control",
"18f0eq": "I restrict food a lot, skip meals, or am afraid of certain foods",
"za9ew6": "Not sure",
"5ct6ya": "Questions about e-cigarettes or a specific medication: it is worth talking to a doctor. We do not recommend them here.",
"1sd91fn": "If during quitting you get chest pain, severe shortness of breath, or thoughts of harming yourself, see a doctor, and in an emergency go to the emergency room or call the ambulance service.",
"9ykb7o": "First of all: worth checking with a doctor",
"1jj6ood": "Most of the studies presented here were done on cigarette smokers, and there is less information on other products. Hookah, e-cigarettes and heated tobacco usually contain nicotine and can create dependence. Support, a quit day and coping with the hard moments are relevant for them too, and you can talk to a doctor.",
"118xtbt": "A slip does not cancel what you have achieved so far, and you can try again. It is worth talking to a counsellor or doctor. One medicine was tested for preventing relapse and found helpful, not all medicines, while an additional behavioural programme alone has not been shown to add anything.",
"1cmzmrz": "There are also prescription medicines for quitting. In reviews they were found effective, and some are more effective than a single form of nicotine replacement, but they have not been shown to be better than combined nicotine replacement. They suit only some people, so this is a conversation with a doctor about suitability and safety. It is an ordinary question, and you can ask it without worry. We do not recommend a specific medicine or dose.",
"1nm1nmc": "Talking to a doctor about medication",
"azblny": "Nicotine replacement (patches, gum, lozenges) raised the chance of quitting by about 1.5 times compared with not using it (a risk ratio). Combining a patch with a fast-acting form worked better than one form. It is worth asking a pharmacist or doctor how to use them.",
"1jn7pv3": "In pregnancy and breastfeeding there is no general recommendation here on nicotine replacement or medicines. The decision is made with a doctor or midwife. Do not start nicotine replacement or a medicine before talking to them. In the meantime, support through talking is a step you can start with.",
"qjl8or": "Based on your answers, it is worth involving a doctor from the start. This is not a diagnosis, but a general recommendation when there are signs of strong nicotine dependence (heavy smoking, first cigarette early in the morning) or when earlier attempts were difficult. If you had severe withdrawal symptoms in the past, it is better not to start alone.",
"1vz2cio": "Worth involving a doctor from the start",
"k7zxws": "With a chronic illness it is worth involving a doctor from the start, so that the steps and treatment fit your situation.",
"9yexvo": "If you take psychiatric medicines or are being treated for a mental health condition, talk to your doctor before you start. After quitting smoking, the blood levels of some medicines may change, so monitoring may be needed. Mood changes may also appear, and your doctor should guide you.",
"dzb2l9": "If you have heart disease, talk to a doctor before nicotine replacement or medicines. In the Cochrane review, more chest pain and palpitations were recorded with nicotine replacement, and they are rare.",
"1bfch7m": "In pregnancy and breastfeeding, quitting smoking matters, and help is available. Support through talking is recommended, and on nicotine replacement and medicines it is worth deciding with a doctor or midwife, because there is currently not enough evidence on the safety or benefit of medicines in pregnancy. It is worth reaching out early, even if quitting is hard.",
"1n6cly": "This coach is for people aged 18 and over. If you are younger and you smoke or use nicotine, it is worth talking to your doctor, a counsellor or an adult you trust. You can quit, and you can get help.",
"8owypw": "Based on your answers. This is general research-based information, not a diagnosis and not a substitute for a doctor. The coach is for people aged 18 and over.",
"pe9rvv": "I take psychiatric medication or am being treated for a mental-health condition",
"1sjmw9u": "According to your answers. This is general research-based information, not a diagnosis and not a substitute for a doctor.",
"mdpbpj": "Each day, write down the time you got into bed and the time you woke up, wake-ups in the night, caffeine and alcohol. After two weeks you can see a pattern and show it to a doctor. Smartwatch data is an estimate, not a medical measurement, and is mainly useful for comparing nights.",
"g1cehv": "Being tired despite enough hours of sleep is a reason to talk to a doctor: it can come from sleep apnea or other conditions that a test can find.",
"4xca52": "A sleep problem that started suddenly, or after an illness or a change in medication, is worth checking with a doctor.",
"16nipvi": "Reducing a dose is a medical decision. If your sleep gets worse during the reduction, tell the doctor who is following you.",
"1o0hh2o": "Do not stop a medication or change a dose without a doctor. Do not add supplements or new medication without checking with them, because there are interactions.",
"mnmjue": "If you are pregnant or breastfeeding, talk to a doctor before taking any supplement or medication for sleep.",
"1ara1o9": "Low mood and anxiety are often linked to poor sleep. It is worth talking to a doctor or a mental health professional, because treating them is part of the solution.",
"1q2as5e": "Sleepiness while driving is an immediate danger. Do not drive when you are sleepy, and see a doctor soon.",
"mfhhao": "I take prescription medication for sleep, anxiety, pain or epilepsy",
"1o2ohbk": "I sleep enough hours but wake up tired",
"5rdhrn": "I sleep little because I go to bed late",
"5i43yp": "I wake up too early and cannot get back to sleep",
"1x1g6jf": "I wake up in the night and find it hard to get back to sleep",
"vi3v10": "If stress persists or affects your sleep or functioning, talk to your doctor or a mental health professional. In the US you can call or text 988; in the UK, Samaritans are on 116 123. In an emergency, call your local emergency number.",
"dys5f9": "According to the American College of Physicians guideline (2016), the first treatment for chronic insomnia is cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT-I), before medication. It is available face to face, in a group, through an app or from a book. A good start is a conversation with your doctor.",
"j2xk60": "The information on this site is general and research-based. It is not medical advice and not a substitute for a doctor. The calculator's estimates are statistical and describe averages of large groups.",
"108wa17": "According to the American College of Physicians guideline (2016), the first treatment for chronic insomnia is cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT-I). A medicine comes into consideration only if CBT-I alone did not work, as a shared decision with a doctor, and for a short period.",
"1194d8g": "I take strong pain medicines, or I use other mind-altering substances",
"1amt6mo": "Mixing alcohol with sedative, sleeping or strong pain medicines, or with other mind-altering substances, can be dangerous even in relatively small amounts. Do not stop a medicine or change a dose without a doctor, and ask your doctor or pharmacist whether it is safe to drink with them.",
"15qeqx5": "What you get here",
"1qyct46": "A personal estimate of how many years you can expect, as a range rather than a single number.",
"1i4klsk": "A picture of the habits that affect the estimate, and what each one changes in it.",
"3s377u": "Free, no sign-up, and the calculation runs in your browser.",
"7p6ebw": "This is a statistical estimate, not a forecast and not a promise.",
"yic6lc": "1 drink = a glass of wine, a small bottle of beer or a shot of spirits, about 14 g of alcohol.",
"jpgjjs": "In an analysis of about 600,000 drinkers (Wood, Lancet 2018), the lowest mortality risk was at up to about 100 grams of pure alcohol a week, roughly 7 standard drinks of about 14 grams of alcohol each (a glass of wine, a small bottle of beer or a shot of spirits), which is about 6 large 175 ml glasses of wine. People who drank more than 350 grams a week lost about 4–5 years of life expectancy at age 40.",
"1401jsg": "And stay within about 7 standard drinks (about 14 grams of alcohol each), or fewer.",
"bqw8h5": "How to start: set a date to quit smoking and tell people close to you, and contact your health fund about a quit-smoking workshop and the medications included in the health basket. For alcohol: count your drinks each week, stay within about 7 standard drinks (about 14 grams of alcohol each), and set a few fixed days a week without alcohol."
});

