Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"xmqso6": "A family history of breast, ovarian or colorectal cancer is not included in the calculation. It mostly concerns your personal risk of the disease itself, and less the average life expectancy. If a first-degree relative was diagnosed before age 50, that is a stronger reason to ask a doctor about earlier screening and genetic counseling.",
"101uzv3": "<p class=\"small muted\"><b>What about healthy years?</b> The calculator estimates life expectancy only, not healthy life expectancy. In a large American study of health professionals (Li et al., BMJ 2020), four or five healthy habits at age 50 were associated with about 8 more years free of cancer, heart disease and diabetes in men, and about 11 years in women. This is an observational link in a different population, and it is not included in the number.</p>",
"19r2hy5": "<b>Healthy years (not in the model):</b> Li Y. et al. Healthy lifestyle and life expectancy free of cancer, cardiovascular disease, and type 2 diabetes: prospective cohort study. <i>BMJ</i> 2020;368:l6669. <span class=\"hrnote\">At age 50, four or five healthy habits: 31.1 disease-free years in men (vs 23.5 with none), 34.4 in women (vs 23.7). Not used in the calculation</span>"
});

Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"kgwgny": "<p class=\"small muted\">The number is life expectancy, not healthy life expectancy. The aim is not only more years but good years, so the result also shows what can be improved.</p>"
});

Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"aetke": "<p class=\"small muted\"><b>What about healthy years?</b> The calculator estimates life expectancy only, not healthy life expectancy. In several large cohort studies in Europe and the United States (Li, BMJ 2020; Stenholm, Int J Epidemiol 2016; Nyberg, JAMA Intern Med 2020), healthy habits such as not smoking, a healthy weight and physical activity were associated with about 5 to 11 more years free of major chronic disease, when comparing the best habits with the worst. These are observational associations, partly among working populations, and not a promise for any individual. Quality of life in the broad sense was not examined in them, and the number here does not include it.</p>",
"1l1fo4x": "<b>Healthy years (not in the model):</b> Stenholm S. et al. Smoking, physical inactivity and obesity as predictors of healthy and disease-free life expectancy between ages 50 and 75: a multicohort study. <i>Int J Epidemiol</i> 2016;45:1260–70. <span class=\"hrnote\">No risk factors vs two or more: about 7.6 (men) and 7.2 (women) more years in good health, and 5.7 and 5.2 years free of chronic disease, between ages 50 and 75. Not used in the calculation</span>",
"bqak7o": "<b>Healthy years (not in the model):</b> Nyberg S.T. et al. Association of Healthy Lifestyle With Years Lived Without Major Chronic Diseases. <i>JAMA Intern Med</i> 2020;180:760–8. <span class=\"hrnote\">Best vs worst lifestyle score: 9.9 more years free of chronic disease in men and 9.4 in women, between ages 40 and 75 (per the abstract). Not used in the calculation</span>"
});

Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"1eptuu7": "The calculator assumes an average smoker. The number of cigarettes and the years of smoking are not asked, and the risk differs a lot between them."
});

Object.assign(I18N.en, {
"1o7pmbf": "<p class=\"small muted\"><b>Years without chronic disease, between ages 50 and 75:</b> In a study from four European countries (Stenholm, Int J Epidemiol 2016), people with these risk factors (",
"3qzeq": "Physical inactivity",
"3hq4qt": "Obesity (BMI 30 or more)",
"1445ith": ") lived on average about ",
"z4ba79": " fewer years without a major chronic disease (heart disease, stroke, cancer, diabetes, lung disease) than people with none of them, who lived about ",
"1mlemxy": " years free of disease out of 25. The effect of several factors does not simply add up, so this is a comparison between profiles and not a value for each factor on its own. It is an observational association, not a promise, and it is not added to the life expectancy number.</p>"
});

